1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,744
MAYA:

2
00:00:00,744 --> 00:00:05,207
So Finn, I’ve done as much as I can for our project on theatre

3
00:00:05,207 --> 00:00:05,691
programmes.

4
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:07,700
How’s your research coming along?

5
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:07,750
FINN:

6
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:08,855
OK, Maya.

7
00:00:08,855 --> 00:00:14,630
I didn’t know theatre programmes are called playbills in the USA till I started

8
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:16,170
looking into the topic.

9
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:16,474
FINN:

10
00:00:16,474 --> 00:00:21,036
Even though I struggled to find many useful websites, I’m glad we picked this

11
00:00:21,036 --> 00:00:21,340
subject.

12
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:25,599
No one else on the course is doing the same as us, although it

13
00:00:25,599 --> 00:00:28,640
is one of the research areas of the module convenor.

14
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:28,928
MAYA:

15
00:00:28,928 --> 00:00:30,940
That might actually put some people off.

16
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:31,369
FINN:

17
00:00:31,369 --> 00:00:32,658
I suppose so.

18
00:00:32,658 --> 00:00:38,670
Anyway, I hadn’t realised there are actually companies specialising in creating theatre programmes.

19
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:39,044
MAYA:

20
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:41,288
Yes, they’re quite common nowadays.

21
00:00:41,288 --> 00:00:46,897
Contrary to what many people think, theatres don’t hire people to do the programmes,

22
00:00:46,897 --> 00:00:52,132
in fact companies buy the rights to publish programmes on the theatre’s behalf –

23
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:56,620
and then make their money selling advertising space within the programme booklet.

24
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:56,846
FINN:

25
00:00:56,846 --> 00:00:59,330
It must be easier for theatres to do it that way.

26
00:00:59,330 --> 00:00:59,885
MAYA:

27
00:00:59,885 --> 00:01:00,440
Yes.

28
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:00,825
FINN:

29
00:01:00,825 --> 00:01:04,675
I remember reading something about programmes in early British theatre.

30
00:01:04,675 --> 00:01:08,140
It said that the cast was always very important.

31
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:08,567
MAYA:

32
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:11,982
Yeah, audiences were very familiar with leading actors,

33
00:01:11,982 --> 00:01:14,970
and big names would draw huge crowds.

34
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:15,291
FINN:

35
00:01:15,291 --> 00:01:19,463
But I hadn’t realised that if the programme named a famous actor,

36
00:01:19,463 --> 00:01:22,030
that’s who the public expected to perform.

37
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:22,346
FINN:

38
00:01:22,346 --> 00:01:27,080
And if that didn’t happen, people accused the theatre of breaking their agreement with

39
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:27,711
the audience.

40
00:01:27,711 --> 00:01:28,974
They would demand refunds,

41
00:01:28,974 --> 00:01:32,130
and, if they didn’t get them, there were riots!

42
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:32,653
MAYA:

43
00:01:32,653 --> 00:01:33,176
Outrageous.

44
00:01:33,176 --> 00:01:35,790
That’d never happen now.

45
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:36,113
FINN:

46
00:01:36,113 --> 00:01:40,966
No, people are too polite, even when they’re disappointed if the star of the

47
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:42,260
show misses a performance.

48
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:42,655
MAYA:

49
00:01:42,655 --> 00:01:47,393
We should definitely include that information about early audiences in our project.

50
00:01:47,393 --> 00:01:53,317
I also think it’s important to mention that lots of ordinary people at that

51
00:01:53,317 --> 00:01:54,501
time were illiterate,

52
00:01:54,501 --> 00:01:58,450
so theatre programmes were of limited value in advertising plays.

53
00:01:58,450 --> 00:01:58,807
MAYA:

54
00:01:58,807 --> 00:02:05,945
When a company of actors arrived in a town, they’d parade around the streets in their costumes, beating drums

55
00:02:05,945 --> 00:02:07,730
and announcing their upcoming performances.

56
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:08,090
FINN:

57
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:08,450
Interesting.

58
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:11,330
I couldn’t imagine that happening now either.

59
00:02:11,330 --> 00:02:11,853
MAYA:

60
00:02:11,853 --> 00:02:18,650
There’s also an interesting comparison to make between eighteenth and nineteenth-century programmes.

61
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:18,995
FINN:

62
00:02:18,995 --> 00:02:23,825
Wasn’t it in the nineteenth century that theatre programmes started to resemble programmes

63
00:02:23,825 --> 00:02:24,170
today?

64
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:24,646
MAYA:

65
00:02:24,646 --> 00:02:25,122
Yes.

66
00:02:25,122 --> 00:02:30,360
And, unlike programmes from the eighteenth century, they always used colour.

67
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:30,822
FINN:

68
00:02:30,822 --> 00:02:34,516
And there was a greater variety of designs.

69
00:02:34,516 --> 00:02:40,518
But personally, I think eighteenth-century programmes were superior because they told the theatre-goers

70
00:02:40,518 --> 00:02:43,750
so many things, including about the actors.

71
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:44,132
MAYA:

72
00:02:44,132 --> 00:02:46,425
And about the writer, the plot,

73
00:02:46,425 --> 00:02:49,100
and sometimes the history of the play.

74
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:49,481
FINN:

75
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:50,623
That’s right.

76
00:02:50,623 --> 00:02:54,810
… What should we say about theatre programmes in the twentieth century?

77
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:55,172
MAYA:

78
00:02:55,172 --> 00:03:00,245
I reckon the most important thing is the dramatic change they underwent during World

79
00:03:00,245 --> 00:03:00,970
War Two.

80
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:01,280
FINN:

81
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,380
When the government imposed restrictions on the use of paper.

82
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:04,805
MAYA:

83
00:03:04,805 --> 00:03:05,231
Yeah,

84
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:08,209
but that was only in the UK.

85
00:03:08,209 --> 00:03:13,314
In the USA, programmes – or rather, playbills – continued to be published in

86
00:03:13,314 --> 00:03:14,590
the same format …

87
00:03:14,590 --> 00:03:14,955
FINN:

88
00:03:14,955 --> 00:03:19,697
… while here in the UK, programmes became merely a single sheet of paper

89
00:03:19,697 --> 00:03:22,250
folded to create four pages for text.

90
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:22,601
MAYA:

91
00:03:22,601 --> 00:03:28,221
What I don’t really get is that, after the war, they didn’t go back

92
00:03:28,221 --> 00:03:30,329
to being more than one sheet,

93
00:03:30,329 --> 00:03:33,490
or change in any way for over twenty-five years.

94
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:33,808
MAYA:

95
00:03:33,808 --> 00:03:36,672
I know there were paper shortages after the war,

96
00:03:36,672 --> 00:03:38,900
but only for five or ten years.

97
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:39,725
FINN:

98
00:03:39,725 --> 00:03:40,550
Strange.

99
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:40,845
MAYA:

100
00:03:40,845 --> 00:03:45,275
I’ve got some pictures of programmes we could include on the slides for our

101
00:03:45,275 --> 00:03:45,570
presentation.

102
00:03:45,570 --> 00:03:45,849
FINN:

103
00:03:45,849 --> 00:03:47,521
I found a couple too, Maya.

104
00:03:47,521 --> 00:03:50,030
Let’s go through and see what we think.

105
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:50,473
MAYA:

106
00:03:50,473 --> 00:03:55,790
Erm …, this is an old one, for a play called Ruy Blas.

107
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:56,192
FINN:

108
00:03:56,192 --> 00:03:57,799
Never heard of that,

109
00:03:57,799 --> 00:04:00,210
but the programme looks very decorative.

110
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:00,567
MAYA:

111
00:04:00,567 --> 00:04:04,133
Good enough to put in a frame on the wall.

112
00:04:04,133 --> 00:04:05,917
The images are just beautiful.

113
00:04:05,917 --> 00:04:07,700
Finn, what did you find?

114
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:08,031
FINN:

115
00:04:08,031 --> 00:04:12,337
I’ve got some pages from a programme for Man of La Mancha.

116
00:04:12,337 --> 00:04:16,975
I thought this was a good programme to show, not because of the pictures,

117
00:04:16,975 --> 00:04:20,950
but because it contains articles written by members of the theatre company.

118
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:21,277
FINN:

119
00:04:21,277 --> 00:04:24,217
So we can learn how the production was created,

120
00:04:24,217 --> 00:04:26,830
and the thoughts and feelings of the cast.

121
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:27,152
MAYA:

122
00:04:27,152 --> 00:04:27,474
Good.

123
00:04:27,474 --> 00:04:31,983
I’ve got a copy of a programme that’s now in a museum.

124
00:04:31,983 --> 00:04:37,135
It’s for The Tragedy of Jane Shore and it’s said to be the earliest

125
00:04:37,135 --> 00:04:41,000
surviving document to have been printed on Australia’s first printing press.

126
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:41,955
FINN:

127
00:04:41,955 --> 00:04:42,910
Fantastic.

128
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:43,274
MAYA:

129
00:04:43,274 --> 00:04:46,912
Another programme to talk about is for The Sailors’ Festival.

130
00:04:46,912 --> 00:04:52,369
It comes from the British Library’s digitised collection of programmes that was started a

131
00:04:52,369 --> 00:04:53,460
few years ago.

132
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:53,953
MAYA:

133
00:04:53,953 --> 00:04:57,900
It already comprises over two hundred thousand programmes,

134
00:04:57,900 --> 00:04:59,380
which is amazing.

135
00:04:59,380 --> 00:04:59,735
FINN:

136
00:04:59,735 --> 00:05:04,000
Wish I’d known about it while I was doing my research!
